photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-GEO-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Plan a one-week wise use challenge for your home or class. Choose three actions, record daily evidence, and write whether it helped and what you would change. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Reduce: buying fewer plastic wraps. Reuse: the jar as a pencil holder. Recycle: cans in the recycling bin. Reasoning: I used reduce by attending to reuse and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking recycling is the only action leaves out that reducing and reusing save more resources before anything is made. Correction: Reduce: buying fewer plastic wraps. Reuse: the jar as a pencil holder. Recycle: cans in the recycling bin. Why: the correction restores accurate use of reduce.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking recycling is the only action leaves out that reducing and reusing save more resources before anything is made. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating reduce on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with reduce, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Reduce: buying fewer plastic wraps. Reuse: the jar as a pencil holder. Recycle: cans in the recycling bin. This matches the lesson skill for reduce.","options":["Thinking recycling is the only action leaves out that reducing and reusing save more resources before anything is made.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Reduce: buying fewer plastic wraps. Reuse: the jar as a pencil holder. Recycle: cans in the recycling bin."],"question":"Sort into reduce, reuse or recycle: buying fewer plastic wraps; using a jar as a pencil holder; putting cans in the recycling bin. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Thinking recycling is the only action leaves out that reducing and reusing save more resources before anything is made. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Thinking recycling is the only action leaves out that reducing and reusing save more resources before anything is made.","Using resources wisely means using less, using things again and recycling materials so fewer new resources are taken. Reduce, reuse, recycle and repair all…","Reduce: buying fewer plastic wraps. Reuse: the jar as a pencil holder. Recycle: cans in the recycling bin.","Careful use of reuse and explain why it matters with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising reduce?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named reuse and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the reuse and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising reduce. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Reduce: buying fewer plastic wraps. Reuse: the jar as a pencil holder. Recycle: cans in the recycling bin. uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Reduce: buying fewer plastic wraps. Reuse: the jar as a pencil holder. Recycle: cans in the recycling bin.","Thinking recycling is the only action leaves out that reducing and reusing save more resources before anything is made.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about reduce?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking recycling is the only action leaves out that reducing and reusing save more resources before anything is made.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about reduce."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about reuse and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Example: a family fixes a dripping tap, takes a short shower and turns off lights in empty rooms. They use a jar as a pencil holder and…","Thinking recycling is the only action leaves out that reducing and reusing save more resources before anything is made."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Using resources wisely means using less, using things again and recycling materials so fewer new resources… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Plan a one-week wise use challenge for your home or class. Choose three actions, record daily evidence, and write whether it helped and what you…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Thinking recycling is the only action leaves out that reducing and reusing save more resources before anything is made."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of reduce?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating reduce on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Reduce: buying fewer plastic wraps. Reuse: the jar as a pencil holder. Recycle: cans in the recycling bin. Reasoning: I used reduce by attending to reuse and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with reduce, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking recycling is the only action leaves out that reducing and reusing save more resources before anything is made. Correction: Reduce: buying fewer plastic wraps. Reuse: the jar as a pencil holder. Recycle: cans in the recycling bin. Why: the correction restores accurate use of reduce.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources"